.TH LIGHTTABLE 1
.SH NAME
lighttable \- a two-dimensional optics bench
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/lighttable
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I LightTable
is a port of the TempleOS
.B Demo/Graphics/LightTable.HC
demonstration.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
Draw mirrors and lenses, then fire photons at them and watch them
reflect and refract.
.PP
Two ideas hold it up.  The optics live in a bitmap rather than in a list
of shapes, so a spline, a freehand line and a flood-filled blob are all
the same thing to a photon.  And the surface normal is recovered from
that bitmap by geometry alone \- sweep an arc each side of the incoming
direction, coarse to fine, for the two points where the surface crosses a
small circle, and take the perpendicular of the chord \- with no stored
gradients and no analytic curve, which is why anything you can draw
refracts correctly.  The fine bitmap is at a larger scale than the
display so that search has sub-pixel detail to work with, which is what
the original's opening comment is about.
.PP
Reflection is one line and refraction is Snell's law with an index of 1.5
inside a lens, falling back to reflection when the angle passes the
critical one.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF "any key"
.TP
.B 0-5
Drawing mode: mirror line, mirror spline, lens line, lens spline, lens
fill, test ray
.TP
.B LMB
Draw, or fire a burst in test-ray mode
.TP
.B .
End a spline
.TP
.B r
Two hundred and fifty-six photons at random
.TP
.B n
Show the normals
.TP
.B Enter
Clear
.TP
.B q
Exit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/lighttable.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR wm (1)
